{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_c3h45qs2boq3kaaqshbwlvye7iosmzlmpjkhdkmxohfakxt5ln6q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## Scope\n\nI first enumerated the full surface rather than assuming. The CMake build compiles `src/lib.c` into one shared library; `nm -D` on the resulting C `.so` exports exactly **one** public symbol:\n\n```\n0000000000001100 T float2half\n```\n\nThe public header `include/lib.h` declares only `uint16_t float2half(float)`, with **no namespace-renaming macros**, so the linker symbol equals the source name. The two 512-entry lookup tables are `static`, hence internal and correctly not exported. So the whole library really is this one function plus its tables — a genuinely small library, not a subset I chose.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- `Cargo.toml` — `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, edition 2024\n- `src/lib.rs` — `M_BASE: [u16; 512]`, `M_SHIFT: [u8; 512]`, and `float2half` with `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern \"C\"`\n\nTo eliminate transcription risk on 1024 hex constants, I generated the tables by parsing the C source programmatically instead of copying them by hand.\n\n## Verification (my own commands, not self-reports)\n\n1. **Symbol diff empty** — `nm -D` on both `.so` files, `diff` clean. Rust exports `float2half`; nothing missing, nothing extra.\n2. **Exhaustive byte-identical output** — a `dlopen` harness ran **all 2^32 bit patterns** (4,294,967,296 inputs) through each library in *separate processes* (deliberately avoiding `dlopen` soname aliasing, since both files share the name `libtranslated_rust.so`), hashing outputs per 9-bit index block. All 512 block hashes matched exactly.\n3. **Negative control** — a stub returning constant `0` produced differing hashes in 409 blocks, and both real libraries yielded 86 distinct block hashes. This confirms the harness is genuinely sensitive and wasn't silently passing.\n4. **Independent table re-parse** — re-extracted both tables from `lib.c` and `lib.rs` in a fresh script and compared element-by-element: 512/512 identical for each.\n5. Clean release build, zero warnings; `c_src/` unmodified (mtimes unchanged).\n\n## Semantics preserved\n\n- The C `union {float; uint32_t}` type-pun became `f32::to_bits()` — both are pure bit reinterpretations, and on the x86-64 SSE ABI no NaN quieting occurs in either language, so signaling NaNs behave identically.\n- `(uint16_t)((uint32_t)base + (mantissa >> shift))` became `base.wrapping_add(mantissa) as u16`, reproducing C's unsigned wraparound and truncating cast rather than panicking in debug or altering results.\n- Shift amounts max at 24, so `>>` on a `u32` is well-defined in both languages — no latent UB to replicate.\n\nNo bugs were \"fixed\": the arithmetic, table values, and truncation behavior are reproduced exactly as written."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":29948,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":706,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4077f296-0fe8-47ae-b2a3-34d905ed7280","uuid":"2fb58fa5-c0c2-4fef-b4b5-666549115e2d","timestamp":"2026-08-19T10:13:46.660Z"}
